  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A few showers/storms tonight but staying warmer\nthan normal into Saturday\n\nModels keep a consistent look with showers making it into WV\nthis evening with some SBCAPES around 1000 J/kg. Overall\nparameters favor some thunderstorms with scattered to numerous\nshowers, but severe threat appears limited. Look for the showers\nto hit the mountains this evening then fall apart/scatter out as\nthey head to the Blue Ridge. Meager amounts of rainfall, with\nlittle to none along/east of the Blue Ridge.\n\nUpper ridge builds back in Friday briefly so not much change in\nairmass just a wind shift with the system tonight into early\nFriday. Temps will be above normal into Saturday, though record\nhighs look out of reach for most. Danville may climb close to\nits record of 93 Saturday.\n\nWith little/no precip and above normal temps/drier airmass, \nfire danger issues remain. See Fire Weather discussion below. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Front Saturday night-Sunday to bring a better\ncoverage of showers with cooldown to normal temperatures to\nstart the workweek.\n\nNext system is stronger aloft and will push in Saturday night\ninto early Sunday. Seems instability will be limited with main\nimpact Sat night, so this may curb thunderstorms somewhat. As we\nhead into Sunday, showers will move through and become more\nscattered. Winds turn west to northwest with gusts to 25 to 30\nmph likely. Cooler temperatures arrive later in the afternoon,\nand could see some snowflakes in the WV mountains Sunday\nevening, though nothing of consequence. \n\nHigh pressure will keep the weather dry until midweek, when \nanother system approaches and looks to bring some showers to \nthe mountains by Thursday but that system also looks limited on\nrainfall amounts. Without much wetting rainfall expected east \nof the Blue Ridge through the next seven days, can anticipate \ndrought conditions to remain or worsen for those areas, and will\nlikely have to monitor for fire weather concerns Monday with \nthe gusty northwesterly winds. See the Fire Weather Section for \nmore details on fire danger through the week.",
